WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.837
Three ways to compare vectors, and when they agree.

2
00:00:03.117 --> 00:00:07.811
You will meet three similarity measures in provider docs and vector databases.

3
00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:11.824
Dot product: the sum of the elementwise products of a and b.

4
00:00:12.104 --> 00:00:14.301
It grows with both alignment and length.

5
00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:19.339
Cosine similarity: the dot product divided by the product of the two norms.

6
00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:26.296
It is the cosine of the angle between the vectors, between -1 and 1, and it ignores length entirely.

7
00:00:26.576 --> 00:00:29.456
Euclidean distance: the norm of a minus b.

8
00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:32.253
Smaller means closer, so you rank ascending.

9
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:35.029
The relationship is one line of algebra.

10
00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:43.459
The squared norm of a minus b equals the squared norm of a plus the squared norm of b minus twice the dot product of a and b.

11
00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:50.373
If every vector has norm 1, that’s 2 minus 2 cos(a, b), and the dot product equals the cosine.

12
00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:54.088
So on unit vectors, all three give identical rankings.

13
00:00:54.368 --> 00:01:03.819
Off the unit sphere they can disagree: the dot product favors long vectors, and Euclidean distance penalizes vectors whose length differs from the query’s.

14
00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.616
This is why provider docs say what they say.

15
00:01:06.896 --> 00:01:24.389
OpenAI’s embeddings guide and the Voyage AI section of Anthropic’s embeddings guide both state that their embeddings are normalized to length 1, so dot product and cosine are the same and Euclidean distance yields the same ranking; the dot product is simply faster.

16
00:01:24.669 --> 00:01:39.987
Google’s Gemini docs say that gemini-embedding-001 vectors truncated below the full 3,072 dimensions must be renormalized by you, while the newer gemini-embedding-2 normalizes truncated outputs itself.

17
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:49.973
Sentence Transformers uses cosine as its default similarity, and its docs recommend the dot product when the model already ends in a normalization layer.

18
00:01:50.253 --> 00:02:00.451
The working rule: use the metric the model was trained and documented for, and if you aren’t sure, normalize vectors once at write time and use the dot product everywhere.

19
00:02:00.731 --> 00:02:05.275
Then your vector store, your offline evals, and your production code all agree.
